NNAAMMEE
       dpkg-parsechangelog - parse Debian changelog files

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddppkkgg--ppaarrsseecchhaannggeelloogg [_o_p_t_i_o_n...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ddppkkgg--ppaarrsseecchhaannggeelloogg reads and parses the changelog of an unpacked
       Debian source tree and outputs the information in it to standard output
       in a machine-readable form.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --ll, ----ffiillee _c_h_a_n_g_e_l_o_g_-_f_i_l_e
           Specifies the changelog file to read information from.  A '-' can
           be used to specify reading from standard input.  The default is
           ddeebbiiaann//cchhaannggeelloogg.

       --FF _c_h_a_n_g_e_l_o_g_-_f_o_r_m_a_t
           Specifies the format of the changelog. By default the format is
           read from a special line near the bottom of the changelog or
           failing that defaults to the ddeebbiiaann standard format. See also
           CCHHAANNGGEELLOOGG FFOORRMMAATTSS.

       --LL _l_i_b_d_i_r
           Obsolete option without effect (since dpkg 1.18.8).  Setting the
           perl environment variables PPEERRLL55LLIIBB or PPEERRLLLLIIBB has a similar effect
           when looking for the parser perl modules.

       --SS, ----sshhooww--ffiieelldd _f_i_e_l_d
           Specifies the name of the field to show (since dpkg 1.17.0).  The
           field name is not printed, only its value.

       --??, ----hheellpp
           Show the usage message and exit.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
           Show the version and exit.

   PPaarrsseerr OOppttiioonnss
       The following options can be used to influence the output of the
       changelog parser, e.g. the range of entries or the format of the
       output.

       ----ffoorrmmaatt _o_u_t_p_u_t_-_f_o_r_m_a_t
           Set the output format. Currently supported values are ddppkkgg and
           rrffcc882222.  ddppkkgg is the classic output format (from before this option
           existed) and the default. It consists of one paragraph in Debian
           control format (see ddeebb--ccoonnttrrooll(5)). If more than one entry is
           requested, then most fields are taken from the first entry (usually
           the most recent entry), except otherwise stated:

           SSoouurrccee:: _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e
           VVeerrssiioonn:: _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
           DDiissttrriibbuuttiioonn:: _t_a_r_g_e_t_-_d_i_s_t_r_i_b_u_t_i_o_n
           UUrrggeennccyy:: _u_r_g_e_n_c_y
               The highest urgency of all included entries is used, followed
               by the concatenated (space-separated) comments from all the
               versions requested.

           MMaaiinnttaaiinneerr:: _a_u_t_h_o_r
           DDaattee:: _d_a_t_e
               The date of the entry as a string, as it appears in the
               changelog.  With a ssttrrppttiimmee(3) format "%%aa,, %%dd %%bb %%YY %%TT %%zz", but
               where the day of the week might not actually correspond to the
               real day obtained from the rest of the date string.  If you
               need a more accurate representation of the date, use the
               TTiimmeessttaammpp field, but take into account it might not be possible
               to map it back to the exact value in this field.

           TTiimmeessttaammpp:: _t_i_m_e_s_t_a_m_p
               The date of the entry as a timestamp in seconds since the epoch
               (since dpkg 1.18.8).

           CClloosseess:: _b_u_g_-_n_u_m_b_e_r
               The Closes fields of all included entries are merged.

           CChhaannggeess:: _c_h_a_n_g_e_l_o_g_-_e_n_t_r_i_e_s
               The text of all changelog entries is concatenated. To make this
               field a valid Debian control format multiline field empty lines
               are replaced with a single full stop and all lines is intended
               by one space character. The exact content depends on the
               changelog format.

           The VVeerrssiioonn, DDiissttrriibbuuttiioonn, UUrrggeennccyy, MMaaiinnttaaiinneerr and CChhaannggeess fields
           are mandatory.

           There might be additional user-defined fields present.

           The rrffcc882222 format uses the same fields but outputs a separate
           paragraph for each changelog entry so that all metadata for each
           entry is preserved.

       ----rreevveerrssee
           Include all changes in reverse order (since dpkg 1.19.1).

           NNoottee:: For the ddppkkgg format the first entry will be the most ancient
           entry.

       ----aallll
           Include all changes.  NNoottee:: Other options have no effect when this
           is in use.

       --ss, ----ssiinnccee _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
       --vv _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
           Include all changes later than _v_e_r_s_i_o_n.

       --uu, ----uunnttiill _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
           Include all changes earlier than _v_e_r_s_i_o_n.

       --ff, ----ffrroomm _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
           Include all changes equal or later than _v_e_r_s_i_o_n.

       --tt, ----ttoo _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
           Include all changes up to or equal than _v_e_r_s_i_o_n.

       --cc, ----ccoouunntt _n_u_m_b_e_r
       --nn _n_u_m_b_e_r
           Include _n_u_m_b_e_r entries from the top (or the tail if _n_u_m_b_e_r is lower
           than 0).

       --oo, ----ooffffsseett _n_u_m_b_e_r
           Change the starting point for ----ccoouunntt, counted from the top (or the
           tail if _n_u_m_b_e_r is lower than 0).

CCHHAANNGGEELLOOGG FFOORRMMAATTSS
       It is possible to use a different format to the standard one, by
       providing a parser for that alternative format.

       In order to have ddppkkgg--ppaarrsseecchhaannggeelloogg run the new parser, a line must be
       included within the last 40 lines of the changelog file, matching the
       Perl regular expression: "\\sscchhaannggeelloogg--ffoorrmmaatt::\\ss++(([[00--99aa--zz]]++))\\WW".  The
       part in parentheses should be the name of the format. For example:

           @@@ changelog-format: _o_t_h_e_r_f_o_r_m_a_t @@@

       Changelog format names are non-empty strings of lowercase alphanumerics
       ("a-z0-9").

       If such a line exists then ddppkkgg--ppaarrsseecchhaannggeelloogg will look for the parser
       as a DDppkkgg::::CChhaannggeelloogg::::_O_t_h_e_r_f_o_r_m_a_t perl module; it is an error for it
       not being present.  The parser name in the perl module will be
       automatically capitalized.  The default changelog format is ddeebbiiaann, and
       a parser for it is provided by default.

       The parser should be derived from the Dpkg::Changelog class and
       implement the required documented interface.

       If the changelog format which is being parsed always or almost always
       leaves a blank line between individual change notes, these blank lines
       should be stripped out, so as to make the resulting output compact.

       If the changelog format does not contain date or package name
       information this information should be omitted from the output. The
       parser should not attempt to synthesize it or find it from other
       sources.

       If the changelog does not have the expected format the parser should
       error out, rather than trying to muddle through and possibly generating
       incorrect output.

       A changelog parser may not interact with the user at all.

NNOOTTEESS
       All PPaarrsseerr OOppttiioonnss except for --vv are only supported since dpkg 1.14.16.

       Short option parsing with non-bundled values available only since dpkg
       1.18.0.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       DDPPKKGG__CCOOLLOORRSS
           Sets the color mode (since dpkg 1.18.5).  The currently accepted
           values are: aauuttoo (default), aallwwaayyss and nneevveerr.

       DDPPKKGG__NNLLSS
           If set, it will be used to decide whether to activate Native
           Language Support, also known as internationalization (or i18n)
           support (since dpkg 1.19.0).  The accepted values are: 00 and 11
           (default).

FFIILLEESS
       ddeebbiiaann//cchhaannggeelloogg
           The changelog file, used to obtain version-dependent information
           about the source package, such as the urgency and distribution of
           an upload, the changes made since a particular release, and the
           source version number itself.

